      "math_academy_id": "q-12453",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Given $P(A|B)=0.3$, $P(A)=0.5$, $P(B)=0.2$, find $P(B|A).$",
      "worked_solution": "Use the multiplication law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap B) & =P(B)\\cdot P(A|B) \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(B|A),$ we use \n\n$$\nP(B|A)=\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|A) & =\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.06}{0.5} \\\\  & =0.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
